Sections "PROJECT" contain missmatched items: {'PROJECT_SUMMARY': ["ABSTRACT Background: Sarcopenic obesity affects up to 40% of older adults with obesity, yet many remain protected despite comparable metabolic burden. The molecular basis for this heterogeneity remains unknown, limiting risk stratification and targeted intervention. Methods: We superimposed metabolic challenge on aging physiology by exposing seventy-two 12-month-old female mice to high-fat diet (HFD) or standard diet for 7 months, modeling the development of sarcopenic obesity during the transition from middle to old age. Multi-Omics Factor Analysis (MOFA) integrated muscle transcriptomics and metabolomics to identify latent factors explaining phenotypic variation. Results: Despite uniform obesity development, aging HFD mice showed marked heterogeneity in muscle outcomes. Functional deficits emerged few weeks following HFD initiation, preceding muscle mass loss and fiber atrophy. Among five latent factors, Factor 3 uniquely distinguished dietary groups and predicted muscle dysfunction independent of adiposity severity. Factor 3 explained 58% of HFD-induced endurance impairment through classic mediation, while revealing suppression effects for muscle mass where metabolic dysfunction counteracted obesity's anabolic drive. Factor 3 revealed a metabolic exhaustion signature characterized by futile adaptation. Fatty acid oxidation genes (Fabp3, Lpl, Acadl, etc.) and oxidative markers were coordinately upregulated. However, this enhanced oxidative potential failed to prevent accumulation of long-chain acylcarnitines and depletion of free L-carnitine, suggesting incomplete β-oxidation and metabolic gridlock. Pathway enrichment analysis revealed concurrent activation of cellular stress responses including p53 signaling, TNF/NFκB, and oxidative stress pathways, indicating comprehensive metabolic decompensation. Plasma acetylcarnitine and pantothenol strongly correlated with muscle Factor 3 signature, achieved AUC 0.91 for discriminating sarcopenia phenotypes. Conclusions: Individual susceptibility to sarcopenic obesity reflects differential activation of metabolic exhaustion rather than obesity severity. This molecular signature of failed oxidative adaptation, detectable through circulating biomarkers, provides a framework for precision risk stratification and early intervention before irreversible muscle loss.", "ABSTRACT Background: Sarcopenic obesity affects up to 40% of older adults with obesity, yet many remain protected despite comparable metabolic burden. The molecular basis for this heterogeneity remains unknown, limiting risk stratification and targeted intervention. Methods: We superimposed metabolic challenge on aging physiology by exposing seventy-two 12-month-old female mice to high-fat diet (HFD) or standard diet for 7 months, modeling the development of sarcopenic obesity during the transition from middle to old age. Multi-Omics Factor Analysis (MOFA) integrated muscle transcriptomics and metabolomics to identify latent factors explaining phenotypic variation. Results: Despite uniform obesity development, aging HFD mice showed marked heterogeneity in muscle outcomes. Functional deficits emerged few weeks following HFD initiation, preceding muscle mass loss and fiber atrophy. Among five latent factors, Factor 3 uniquely distinguished dietary groups and predicted muscle dysfunction independent of adiposity severity. Factor 3 explained 58% of HFD-induced endurance impairment through classic mediation, while revealing suppression effects for muscle mass where metabolic dysfunction counteracted obesity''s anabolic drive. Factor 3 revealed a metabolic exhaustion signature characterized by futile adaptation. Fatty acid oxidation genes (Fabp3, Lpl, Acadl, etc.) and oxidative markers were coordinately upregulated. However, this enhanced oxidative potential failed to prevent accumulation of long-chain acylcarnitines and depletion of free L-carnitine, suggesting incomplete β-oxidation and metabolic gridlock. Pathway enrichment analysis revealed concurrent activation of cellular stress responses including p53 signaling, TNF/NFκB, and oxidative stress pathways, indicating comprehensive metabolic decompensation. Plasma acetylcarnitine and pantothenol strongly correlated with muscle Factor 3 signature, achieved AUC 0.91 for discriminating sarcopenia phenotypes. Conclusions: Individual susceptibility to sarcopenic obesity reflects differential activation of metabolic exhaustion rather than obesity severity. This molecular signature of failed oxidative adaptation, detectable through circulating biomarkers, provides a framework for precision risk stratification and early intervention before irreversible muscle loss."]}
